'''basis''' (bā′sis), ''n.''; pl. ''bases'' (-sēz). [L., < Gr. βάσις, a going, step, foundation: see ''base''<sup>2</sup>.] '''1.''' The foundation of anything; that on which a thing stands or on which anything is reared; a foundation, groundwork, or supporting principle: now most commonly used of immaterial things.
{{smaller block|Build me thy fortunes upon the ''basis'' of valour. Challenge me the Count’s youth to fight with him; hurt him in eleven places.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. N., iii. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Who builds a monument, the ''basis'' jasper,
And the main body brick?</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Mad Lover, iv. 4.}}}}
{{smaller block|Good health is the ''basis'' of all physical, intellectual, moral, and spiritual development.
{{right|''J. F. Clarke'', Self-Culture, ii.}}}}
'''2.''' In ''arch.'', same as ''base''<sup>2</sup>, 3.—'''3†.''' A pedestal.
{{smaller block|Observing an English inscription upon the ''basis'', we read it over several times.
{{right|''Addison.''}}}}
'''4.''' The principal constituent of a compound; a fundamental ingredient.—'''5.''' ''Milit.'', same as ''base''<sup>2</sup>, 15 (''a'').—'''6.''' In ''crystal.'' and ''petrog.'', same as ''basal plane'' (which see, under ''basal'').—'''7.''' In ''bot.'' and ''conch.'', same as ''base''<sup>2</sup>, 4.—'''8.''' [NL.] In ''anat.'', the base; the fundamental or basilar part of anything: as, ''basis'' cranii, the base of the skull.—'''9.''' In ''pros.'', a trochee or its substitute preceding the dactyls of a logaœdic series. {{smaller|An apparent spondee or iambus, a long syllable of three times, or even a pyrrhic, tribrach, or anapest, may be used as a basis, and an anacrusis may be prefixed to it. The basis is sometimes double. [This meaning of the word is of modern introduction (Gottfried Hermann). In ancient Greek writers on metrics the meaning of βάσις is: (''a'') That part of the foot which takes the σημασία (ictus); the θέσις. (''b'') A series of}}
